### NON-DEFAULT stacking ports only -- deliberately EMPTY.
### FINDING 2026-07-30: on the IE520, port1.0.27/1.0.28 are DEDICATED stackports that
### could NOT be converted to network ports. `no stackport` was accepted and saved, but
### after reboot the flag came back on the real member's ports (it only stuck on the
### absent member's phantom ports); `show interface brief` still reports
### "port1.0.27 admin up stackport down". Consistent with `stackport` not appearing in
### ck.db's IE5xx command-validity table. Likewise `stack virtual-chassis-id` has NO `no`
### form (`no stack ?` offers only <1-8>, all, disabled-master-monitoring, management,
### resiliencylink, virtual-mac), so both units still carry `stack virtual-chassis-id 3039`.
### !! DO NOT CABLE port1.0.27/1.0.28 BETWEEN swi_a AND swi_b. Both units are now stack
###    ID 1 sharing chassis-id 3039, so a stackport link would make both claim ID 1 and
###    drop straight back into the duplicate-master / err-disabled state just cleaned up.
[configured_stackport]

### Maps each device to its [power] outlet -- what lets a test power-cycle a device.
### swi_a/swi_b ADDED 2026-08-11 when both IE520s were fitted to the PDU.
### Worth knowing why this matters more than it looks: a device with no powerlink does
### not fail fast. ATSwitch.__power() logs "Power object is not available" and returns
### False WITHOUT raising, so a suite calling dut.off() carries on and then waits for a
### bootloader banner that can never appear -- at AWPConsoleCore's flat 1800 s default.
[powerlink]
swi_a = pwr_a
swi_b = pwr_b
swi_c = pwr_c
swi_d = pwr_d

[boot_from_flash]
swi_a = True
swi_b = True
swi_c = True
swi_d = True

